1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,700
Your AI agent just told a customer they can get a refund anytime.

2
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:04,800
That's wrong.

3
00:00:04,820 --> 00:00:06,780
Your policy says thirty days.

4
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:09,040
And yet, your dashboard is green.

5
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:10,060
No errors.

6
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:11,000
No crashes.

7
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:13,200
Just a confident, wrong answer.

8
00:00:13,220 --> 00:00:15,620
So how do you find out why it happened?

9
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:18,350
That's the job of AI observability.

10
00:00:19,214 --> 00:00:21,804
Monitoring tells you whether something is broken.

11
00:00:22,334 --> 00:00:24,004
Observability tells you why.

12
00:00:24,534 --> 00:00:30,004
A system is observable when you can understand what's happening inside it, just from the data it emits.

13
00:00:30,554 --> 00:00:32,684
For traditional software, that's useful.

14
00:00:33,134 --> 00:00:34,744
For AI, it's essential.

15
00:00:35,264 --> 00:00:38,414
Because AI breaks the old rules in three ways.

16
00:00:38,804 --> 00:00:39,844
First, it's non-deterministic.

17
00:00:39,864 --> 00:00:42,864
The same question can get a different answer every time.

18
00:00:42,884 --> 00:00:45,484
Second, agents are chains of decisions.

19
00:00:45,504 --> 00:00:50,964
One request might plan, search your documents, call two tools, and call the model three times.

20
00:00:50,984 --> 00:00:53,644
A mistake at any step ruins the final answer.

21
00:00:53,664 --> 00:00:57,004
And third, every request costs money, measured in tokens.

22
00:00:57,024 --> 00:00:59,204
A bug in a normal app makes it slow.

23
00:00:59,224 --> 00:01:01,574
A bug in an agent can make it expensive.

24
00:01:02,114 --> 00:01:04,604
Observability rests on four ideas.

25
00:01:05,334 --> 00:01:06,884
Let's follow one small agent.

26
00:01:07,294 --> 00:01:10,664
It answers: what's the weather in Paris, and do I need an umbrella?

27
00:01:11,554 --> 00:01:12,454
Logs are timestamped events.

28
00:01:12,474 --> 00:01:13,754
Request received.

29
00:01:13,774 --> 00:01:16,064
Weather tool timed out, retrying.

30
00:01:16,354 --> 00:01:17,974
Lots of detail.

31
00:01:17,994 --> 00:01:21,664
But with thousands of users, it's hard to tell which lines belong together.

32
00:01:22,412 --> 00:01:24,722
Metrics are numbers over time.

33
00:01:25,392 --> 00:01:25,962
Requests per minute.

34
00:01:26,712 --> 00:01:28,202
Ninety-fifth percentile latency.

35
00:01:29,072 --> 00:01:29,642
Tokens used.

36
00:01:30,352 --> 00:01:31,182
Dollars spent today.

37
00:01:31,632 --> 00:01:33,582
They're perfect for dashboards and alerts.

38
00:01:33,772 --> 00:01:37,642
But they tell you that something changed, not which request changed.

39
00:01:38,270 --> 00:01:39,891
Traces connect it all.

40
00:01:40,461 --> 00:01:45,091
A trace follows one request from start to finish, under a single trace ID.

41
00:01:45,701 --> 00:01:48,631
And every step inside that trace is a span.

42
00:01:49,121 --> 00:01:50,551
The document search is a span.

43
00:01:50,841 --> 00:01:52,231
The weather tool is a span.

44
00:01:52,581 --> 00:01:58,991
The model call is a span, carrying the model name, input and output tokens, cost, and duration.

45
00:02:00,041 --> 00:02:02,751
Spans nest inside each other, so you see the whole tree.

46
00:02:03,235 --> 00:02:05,665
So how does that data leave your app?

47
00:02:06,095 --> 00:02:07,945
Through OpenTelemetry, or OTel.

48
00:02:08,355 --> 00:02:09,735
It's an open, vendor-neutral standard.

49
00:02:09,755 --> 00:02:12,855
Your code creates spans with the OTel SDK.

50
00:02:12,875 --> 00:02:23,605
They're batched in the background and shipped over a protocol called OTLP, either straight to a backend, or through a Collector that can filter, redact, and route them.

51
00:02:23,989 --> 00:02:28,949
For AI, OTel adds the GenAI semantic conventions.

52
00:02:28,969 --> 00:02:36,369
Shared names, like gen_ai.request.model, and gen_ai.usage.input_tokens, so every tool reads your data the same way.

53
00:02:36,389 --> 00:02:37,329
Instrument once.

54
00:02:37,349 --> 00:02:38,799
Send it anywhere.

55
00:02:39,295 --> 00:02:41,425
In practice, it takes a few lines.

56
00:02:42,175 --> 00:02:51,325
Wrap your model client, decorate the functions you care about, and every call becomes a span, with its inputs, outputs, tokens, and cost recorded for you.

57
00:02:52,105 --> 00:02:53,185
So what should you watch?

58
00:02:53,205 --> 00:02:54,415
Latency.

59
00:02:54,865 --> 00:03:00,715
Not the average, but the ninety-fifth percentile, and time to first token, which is what users actually feel.

60
00:03:01,505 --> 00:03:04,715
Errors, like rate limits, timeouts, and failed tools.

61
00:03:05,245 --> 00:03:08,715
Tokens and cost, per request, per user, and per feature.

62
00:03:09,225 --> 00:03:12,675
And quality, from user feedback or automated evaluations.

63
00:03:13,285 --> 00:03:20,115
Then tag everything with the user, the session, and the prompt version, so after every release you can answer one question.

64
00:03:20,545 --> 00:03:22,475
Did yesterday's change make things worse?

65
00:03:23,203 --> 00:03:26,013
You don't have to build any of this yourself.

66
00:03:26,763 --> 00:03:33,593
LangSmith and Langfuse are built for LLM apps, and Langfuse is open source, so you can run it on your own laptop.

67
00:03:34,423 --> 00:03:37,503
Arize Phoenix is another open option.

68
00:03:37,523 --> 00:03:41,843
Datadog and Dynatrace put your AI traces right next to your infrastructure.

69
00:03:41,863 --> 00:03:45,963
They use different words: runs, observations, spans.

70
00:03:45,983 --> 00:03:48,543
But the ideas are the same.

71
00:03:48,563 --> 00:03:53,193
And more and more, so is the foundation underneath them: OpenTelemetry.

72
00:03:53,605 --> 00:03:55,407
Back to that wrong refund answer.

73
00:03:55,837 --> 00:03:58,067
Open the trace, and you see it in seconds.

74
00:03:58,637 --> 00:04:01,467
The retrieval step pulled an outdated policy document.

75
00:04:02,037 --> 00:04:02,607
No guessing.

76
00:04:03,197 --> 00:04:04,647
That's AI observability.

77
00:04:05,297 --> 00:04:08,747
Every step, every token, every decision, visible.
